{% extends 'help/article.html.twig' %}

{% block body %}
    <p>
        Maintenance has two halves: <strong>schedules</strong>, which say when something is due,
        and <strong>records</strong>, which say what was actually done. Schedules are set once and
        mostly left alone; records accumulate as work happens.
    </p>

    <h2>Schedules</h2>
    <p>
        A schedule is a service type plus an interval. Set it <strong>fleet-wide</strong> and it
        applies to every truck; set one on a <strong>specific truck</strong> and it overrides the
        fleet default for that truck only.
    </p>
    <p>
        That two-level arrangement is the point. Oil every 25,000 miles across the fleet, every
        15,000 on the one truck that runs mountain grades all day — one override, no exceptions
        list to maintain.
    </p>
    <p>An interval can be set in:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Miles</strong> — service every <em>n</em> miles since the last one.</li>
        <li><strong>Days</strong> — service every <em>n</em> days.</li>
        <li><strong>Both</strong> — whichever arrives first, which is usually what you want.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Each schedule also has an alert threshold — how many miles or days ahead it should start
        warning you. The defaults are 1,000 miles and 7 days.
    </p>

    <h2>Due states</h2>
    <p>Every truck and schedule combination resolves to one of three states:</p>
    <div class="help-scroll">
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr><th>State</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><strong>On track</strong></td><td>Outside the alert threshold.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Due soon</strong></td><td>Inside the threshold — within the next 1,000 miles or 7 days, by default.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Overdue</strong></td><td>Past the interval.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p>
        These are <strong>worked out every time you look</strong>, against the truck's current
        odometer and today's date. Nothing is stored and nothing has to be recalculated on a
        schedule, so a state is never stale — and there is no flag anyone can forget to clear.
    </p>

    <div class="warn">
        <p>Mileage-based intervals are only as good as the odometer. With an
        <a href="{{ path('help_article', { slug: 'integrations' }) }}">ELD connected</a> that number
        updates itself; without one you are typing it in, and a truck whose reading has not been
        updated in two months will not come due when it should.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>The due list</h2>
    <p>
        The maintenance view shows the whole fleet ordered worst-first — overdue at the top, then
        due soon — with miles and days remaining for each. It is built to be read top-down in the
        morning and stopped at when you reach the trucks that are fine.
    </p>

    <h2>Records</h2>
    <p>A record is one piece of work, and comes in three kinds:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Service</strong> — scheduled preventive work.</li>
        <li><strong>Repair order</strong> — something that broke.</li>
        <li><strong>Estimate</strong> — quoted but not yet approved.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Each moves through <strong>scheduled → in progress → completed</strong>, or
        <strong>cancelled</strong>. There are thirteen service types — oil change, brake
        inspection, tyre rotation and replacement, DOT inspection, PM service, DEF/emissions,
        transmission, coolant, air filter, alignment, body work and other.
    </p>
    <p>
        A record carries the vendor, work order and invoice numbers, the odometer at the time, an
        estimated cost and the actual cost. Recording the odometer on the record is what resets
        the interval, so do not skip it.
    </p>

    <h2>Costs reach the ledger by themselves</h2>
    <p>
        A completed record with a cost creates a matching line on the
        <a href="{{ path('help_article', { slug: 'expenses' }) }}">expense ledger</a>
        automatically, and keeps it in step if the cost changes. You do not enter maintenance
        spend twice, and fleet cost totals include the shop without anyone re-keying invoices.
    </p>

    <h2>Vendor spend</h2>
    <p>
        Work is grouped by shop: how many invoices, total spent, the average, and how many
        estimates are still open. Filter it by year. The open-estimates figure is the one worth
        watching — it is quoted work that has neither been approved nor declined.
    </p>
{% endblock %}
